    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d185">

                <pb facs="0355" n="259" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg259"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/254a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chairman of the American Delegation</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract—Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-11-13T15:00:00-05:00">November 13, 1934—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>17.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>We are convinced by the conversations which have taken place over the past three
                        weeks<anchor n="6" xml:id="d185-f1"/> that practically no chance exists of
                    bridging the definite disagreement between the Japanese delegation on the one
                    hand and the British and ourselves on the other with regard to the fundamentals
                    of future naval limitation. Every opportunity has been afforded the Japanese to
                    explain and to justify Japan’s demands; we have not forced the pace and we have
                    not refused them a chance to “save face”. We should continue to emphasize our
                    thesis that maintaining the treaties as a basis for future naval limitation
                    rests on the equality of self-defense, equality of security, and on a united
                    purpose to avoid competition in armaments. The only construction we can place on
                    the Japanese thesis is that it represents a desire to obtain overwhelming
                    supremacy in the Orient opening the way <pb facs="0356" n="260" xml:id="pg260"/>
                    to preferential rights and privileges and destroying the delicate balance in
                    Asia, both economic and political, which is represented by the other basic
                    principles and policies that are embodied in the Washington and other
                    treaties.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
